Extension API: the fleet screen's view state on the wire ("FLTS").

STATUS: applied locally to ~/orion2re (branch orionlayer-local, commit
cc5ec133) by work order 134 C, 19 September 2026. Required by
tools/version_check.py. NOT CONFIRMED LIVE — work order 134's live part
is parked (doc/briefs/134-parked-for-data.md); it compiles and the
client parses it, and that is all that has been shown. It comes back off
with

    cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
        ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_fleet_screen_state.patch

then rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    FLT1::Fleet_Screen_ (flt1.cpp:486-837) keeps everything it shows in
    MOX and FLT1 globals (mox.cpp:109-188, flt1.cpp:5-12), and
    SerializeState touched none of them. Without this block an HD client
    knows the screen is up (ext::Tick reports 4) and knows the field
    list, and nothing else: not which stack is shown, not which ships
    are in the grid, not which of them are selected, not where the list
    is scrolled, not which of the two filters are on.

    RECONSTRUCTION WAS TRIED FIRST (decision 25 of the OrionLayer
    fundament) and it does not reach. The big-icon MEMBERSHIP is the
    hard part:

      - FSEL (open fix 20 revision 2) sends the whole node table, but
        the only CHAIN it sends is the fleet box's, and that is -1 here:
        the fleet screen closes box 2 (flt1.cpp:826-832). _ship_stack_-
        start[] and _ship_node[].next_node are not on the wire at all.
      - Walking the node table instead names ships the player cannot
        see: SHIPSTAK::Remove_Non_Detected_Ships_ (shipstak.cpp:200-250)
        unlinks nodes of foreign stacks and Delete_Ship_Node_ (:5-11)
        leaves their ship_idx in place.
      - Grouping own ships by s_ship.location would be a different list
        from the engine's in both membership and order, and would have
        no way to say so.

    The one thing a client CAN see is how many big-icon fields the
    screen added, because they are in the field list at known grid
    rects. That says how many ships there are and where their cells sit.
    It does not say WHICH ships, which is what the panel, the icons and
    every command need.

WHAT IT DOES

    Appends an optional trailing block, written only while
    MOX::_current_screen == SCREEN_FLEET and LAST in the snapshot —
    after FSEL, so nothing above it moves and an older client that stops
    reading earlier is unaffected:

        'F','L','T','S'
        int16  _small_ship_stack_ptr          (-1 if out of range)
        int16  _ship_stack_start[ptr]         (-1 with no stack)
        int16  _fltscrn_stack_owner
        int16  _n_fltscrn_big_icons           (clamped)
        int16  _n_fltscrn_big_icons_added
        int16  _n_fltscrn_rows
        int16  _scroll_bar->first_visible_row (0 while null)
        int16  _n_big_icons_selected
        int16  _scanned_big_ship
        int16  _scanned_small_ship
        int16  _fltscrn_support_button_status
        int16  _fltscrn_combat_button_status
        uint8  FLT1::_relocate_button_mode
        uint8  FLT1::_merging_relocations
        int16  N
        N x  ( int16 ship_idx, uint8 selected )

    The list is a snapshot of _fltscrn_big_icon[] in DISPLAY order, not
    a rebuild: that is the order Build_Fltscrn_Big_Icon_Fields_ left
    there (flt2.cpp:207-268, including the officer move-to-front at
    :263-265), and the order Get_Fltscrn_Big_Icon_XY_ turns into grid
    positions (flt2.cpp:116-128).

    Nothing is dereferenced without a check: the count is clamped to
    SHIP_LIST_CAPACITY, a null _fltscrn_big_icon writes -1 entries
    instead of reading, and _scroll_bar is read as 0 while it is still
    null (the pointer is set only once a list exists, flt1.cpp:1245-1248).

COST IF IT IS NOT APPLIED

    Nothing changes for the game: the block is written and never read by
    it. For a client, the HD fleet screen cannot draw its grid, its ship
    panel or its filters, and says so and hands over to the original
    picture instead (decision 22).

diff --git a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
index 362ccf43..d1010bb7 100644
--- a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
@@ -239,6 +239,81 @@ static void SerializeState(std::vector<uint8_t>& buf,
             n = MOX::_ship_node[n].next_node;
         }
     }
+
+    // 3. The fleet screen's view state: "FLTS", written ONLY while
+    //    SCREEN_FLEET is up, and LAST, so nothing above it moves and a
+    //    client that stops after FSEL is unaffected. OrionLayer, open
+    //    fix 27.
+    //
+    //    NOTHING IN THIS BLOCK IS REACHABLE ANY OTHER WAY. Every value
+    //    is a MOX or FLT1 global (mox.cpp:109-188, flt1.cpp:5-12) that
+    //    SerializeState never touched, and the one that matters most —
+    //    WHICH ships the screen is showing, in the order it shows them
+    //    — cannot be rebuilt from what is already sent. FSEL carries
+    //    the node table but only box 2's chain, which is -1 here
+    //    because the fleet screen closes that box; and walking the node
+    //    table instead does not work, because Remove_Non_Detected_Ships_
+    //    (shipstak.cpp:200-250) unlinks nodes of foreign stacks while
+    //    Delete_Ship_Node_ (:5-11) leaves their ship_idx in place, so a
+    //    client walking the table names ships the player cannot see.
+    //
+    //    The list is written in DISPLAY order, which is the order
+    //    Build_Fltscrn_Big_Icon_Fields_ left in _fltscrn_big_icon
+    //    (flt2.cpp:207-268, including the officer move-to-front at
+    //    :263-265) — the same order Get_Fltscrn_Big_Icon_XY_ turns into
+    //    grid positions. It is a snapshot of an array, not a rebuild.
+    if (current_screen == SCREEN_FLEET) {
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'F');
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'L');
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'T');
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'S');
+
+        int16_t stack = MOX::_small_ship_stack_ptr;
+        int16_t head = -1;
+        if (stack >= 0 && stack < MOX::_ship_stack_count) {
+            head = MOX::_ship_stack_start[stack];
+        } else {
+            stack = -1;
+        }
+        Write16(buf, stack);
+        Write16(buf, head);
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_fltscrn_stack_owner);
+
+        int16_t icons = MOX::_n_fltscrn_big_icons;
+        if (icons < 0) icons = 0;
+        if (icons > SHIP_LIST_CAPACITY) icons = SHIP_LIST_CAPACITY;
+        Write16(buf, icons);
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_n_fltscrn_big_icons_added);
+        Write16(buf, (int16_t)MOX::_n_fltscrn_rows);
+
+        // first_visible_row lives in the scroll bar the screen points
+        // MOX::_scroll_bar at (flt1.cpp:1245-1248); the pointer is null
+        // until the first list is built, and 0 is then the truth.
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_scroll_bar ? MOX::_scroll_bar->first_visible_row
+                                      : (int16_t)0);
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_n_big_icons_selected);
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_scanned_big_ship);
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_scanned_small_ship);
+
+        // The two filter radios. Their STATUS is what the screen reads
+        // (flt2.cpp:130-146); the fields carry it by pointer and it is
+        // not in the field list.
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_fltscrn_support_button_status);
+        Write16(buf, MOX::_fltscrn_combat_button_status);
+        Write8(buf, FLT1::_relocate_button_mode);
+        Write8(buf, FLT1::_merging_relocations);
+
+        Write16(buf, icons);
+        for (int16_t i = 0; i < icons; i++) {
+            if (MOX::_fltscrn_big_icon) {
+                Write16(buf, MOX::_fltscrn_big_icon[i].ship_idx);
+                Write8(buf, (uint8_t)(MOX::_fltscrn_big_icon[i].selected != 0));
+            } else {
+                Write16(buf, (int16_t)-1);
+                Write8(buf, (uint8_t)0);
+            }
+        }
+    }
 }
 
